WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.640
President Reagan loved it, test&nbsp;
audiences were puzzled by it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.320
and Princess Diana was at the&nbsp;
World Premiere? Great Scott!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:14.160
Seeing Back to the Future in 1985 sure&nbsp;
sounds like a trip. Let's go back in time!

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.920
Some films take a while to find their audience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.000
Even movies that are later considered&nbsp;
classics don't always receive a warm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.960
reception at the box office or enjoy&nbsp;
favorable reviews when they first come out.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.080
But not "Back to the Future." From the&nbsp;
start, film reviewers from major publications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.040
like The New York Times and Variety&nbsp;
all gave the movie glowing reviews.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.120
Some, like Newsweek's Jack&nbsp;
Kroll, called the film, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:47.760
"the smartest, sweetest, funniest comedy in&nbsp;
many summers." Others, like Empire's Adam Smith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.200
praised the screenplay — an observation that&nbsp;
would be echoed in film classes for decades.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:58.080
Film critic Roger Ebert was particularly&nbsp;
complimentary, giving the flick 3 and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.520
half out of 4 stars. In his review, Ebert&nbsp;
compared the film to the all-time classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:08.640
"It's a Wonderful Life," praising the hopeful&nbsp;
way it allowed audiences to see their parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:14.160
in a new light. Director Robert Zemeckis also&nbsp;
received accolades from the legendary critic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.920
with Ebert claiming that his touch gave the film,&nbsp;
quote, "charm, brains, and a lot of laughter."

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:26.240
Considering that the project was turned down&nbsp;
40 times by multiple studios before being made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.560
the warm critical reception to "Back to&nbsp;
the Future" had to be extremely gratifying.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.120
One of the funniest moments from “Back to&nbsp;
the Future” came when the Doc Brown of 1955&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:43.040
asked Marty who the President of the United&nbsp;
States was in 1985 — and then reacted with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.960
disbelief when he heard that actor Ronald Reagan&nbsp;
would become President Reagan in the future.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.480
"Who's president of the United States in 1985?"

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.160
"Ronald Reagan!"

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.000
"Ronald Reagan? The actor?!”

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.000
According to his former speechwriter Mark&nbsp;
Weinberg, Reagan, who viewed the film for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.400
the first time at Camp David, "got a kick"&nbsp;
out of an early scene where Marty walked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.400
by a 1955 movie theater advertising the&nbsp;
Reagan film, "Cattle Queen of Montana."

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:18.000
When Doc had his famous exchange with Marty,&nbsp;
however, the mood at Camp David darkened — not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.080
because of Doc's comment about Reagan, but&nbsp;
because the character then sarcastically asked,

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.720
"I suppose Jane Wyman is the first lady?"

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:36.240
Wyman was Reagan's first wife, whom he divorced in&nbsp;
1949. By 1955, he was married to future First Lady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:42.400
Nancy Reagan. While the movie completely ignored&nbsp;
that fact, Reagan didn't. According to Weinberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.360
there was a, quote, "unspoken ban" on&nbsp;
mentioning Wyman among the Reagan staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.440
making the film's reference to&nbsp;
her a very uncomfortable moment.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.920
Despite his brief discomfort at the&nbsp;
mention of ex-wife Jane Wyman by Doc Brown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.440
President Ronald Reagan became a&nbsp;
big fan of "Back to the Future."

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.880
His speechwriter Weinberg would&nbsp;
later recall that both Ronald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.000
and Nancy Reagan were impressed by Michael&nbsp;
J. Fox's performance as Marty McFly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.240
This may have been related to the fact&nbsp;
that Fox was also playing Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:19.920
teenager Alex Keaton on the Reagans' favorite&nbsp;
television show at the time, "Family Ties."

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.040
Weinberg said that Reagan remarked&nbsp;
about 'Back to the Future,"

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.480
"This was the type of movie that&nbsp;
Hollywood should be making."

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.000
Six months later, President Reagan even&nbsp;
quoted "Back to the Future" in his February 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.920
1986 State of the Union address. Encouraging&nbsp;
the American people to look to the skies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.880
as the limit to their achievements, Reagan stated,

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:46.480
"As they said in the film Back to the Future,&nbsp;
'Where we're going, we don't need roads."

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.400
Zemeckis ultimately chose to use a&nbsp;
DeLorean as the basis for his time machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:58.000
partly because the gull-wing doors of the&nbsp;
car would allow a 1955 family to mistake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.920
the vehicle for an alien spacecraft when Marty&nbsp;
crashes into their barn. Although Ford offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.720
the production team money if they made the&nbsp;
time machine out of a Mustang, co-writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.640
Bob Gale reportedly responded with, quote,&nbsp;
"Doc Brown doesn't drive a f–--ing Mustang."

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:21.360
The time machine's stylish and memorable design&nbsp;
earned the filmmakers praise from the sports car's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:27.920
original designer — John DeLorean. By 1982, the&nbsp;
DeLorean Motor Company had gone bankrupt and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:34.080
factory closed. This ended DeLorean's dream&nbsp;
of creating a, quote, "ethical sports car."

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:40.160
Thanks to “Back to the Future," however, the&nbsp;
DeLorean DMC-12's image was immortalized.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.720
Realizing the movie had kept his dream alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.040
John DeLorean sent the filmmakers a fan&nbsp;
letter thanking them for using his car.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.480
John DeLorean wasn't the only fan of&nbsp;
"Back to the Future" that loved the car.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:59.040
The film fueled a major DeLorean collecting&nbsp;
craze that persists to this day. Reportedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:05.440
the DeLorean Motor Company only managed to&nbsp;
produce over 9000 DMC-12’s before the company went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:13.280
bankrupt in 1982. However, thanks in part to "Back&nbsp;
to the Future," an estimated 6,500 DeLorean cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:19.200
are still on the road today. This is in sharp&nbsp;
contrast to other discontinued car models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:23.040
which normally phase out of&nbsp;
existence once they stop being made.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:30.080
Ironically, in 1984, former DeLorean dealer Steve&nbsp;
Lopinto claimed the car, quote, "doesn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:35.760
enough mystique to become a collector's car."&nbsp;
Flash forward to 2011 when someone purchased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:42.640
an original "Back to the Future" DeLorean for&nbsp;
$541,000. Thankfully, DeLoreans that didn't appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:51.840
in the film are much more affordable — a 1981&nbsp;
DMC-12 went for $23,100 at an auction in 2019.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.640
Of course, many DeLorean car collectors&nbsp;
don't just want a classic sports car — they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:00.560
want the time machine from “Back to&nbsp;
the Future.” This led many fans to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:05.440
modify their DeLoreans and make them&nbsp;
resemble Doc Brown's iconic design.

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:08.480
Among these builders are Oliver and Terry Holler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:13.360
who spent three and a half months refitting&nbsp;
a DeLorean sports car with scrap metal and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.160
surplus electronics to recreate the&nbsp;
"Back to the Future" time machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:23.680
Today, the Hollers volunteer for the Michael&nbsp;
J. Fox Foundation for Parkinson's Research.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.240
They aren't the only DeLorean fanatics.&nbsp;
In the documentary "Back in Time,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:34.160
fan Joe Walser recalled that he promised he would&nbsp;
buy a DeLorean and turn it into a time machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:39.600
Today, Walser is on the Time Machine Restoration&nbsp;
Team and helped restore one of the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:44.960
DeLorean time machines after it fell into&nbsp;
disrepair on the Universal Studios Movie Lot.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:50.160
If you look closely at the dates&nbsp;
on Doc Brown's time machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.920
you'll see that Marty McFly first drove&nbsp;
the time-traveling DeLorean on October 26,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:02.320
1985 in the parking lot of Twin Pines Mall,&nbsp;
which was actually Puente Hills Mall in City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:07.760
of Industry, California. However, "Back to the&nbsp;
Future" was released to the public on July 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.000
1985 — almost four months before&nbsp;
the film's events took place.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:19.760
This led a group of fans to show up at Puente&nbsp;
Hills Mall on October 26, 1985, to see if Marty or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:25.120
Doc Brown would appear in the famous DeLorean.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, neither character made a real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:29.840
appearance — but that didn't stop another group&nbsp;
of fans from showing up at the Puente Hills Mall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:36.720
again in October 2015 to see if Doc or Marty&nbsp;
would suddenly appear 30 years later. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:41.760
the owner of a DeLorean time machine replica&nbsp;
was on location to give fans a fun photo op.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:49.760
In the documentary "Back in Time," Michael J. Fox&nbsp;
recalled that during the 1985 world premiere of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:55.360
“Back to the Future,” he was seated next to none&nbsp;
other than Princess Diana, who was attending the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:01.440
screening with her then-husband Prince Charles.&nbsp;
Prior to the event, Fox and the other attendees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:06.160
received a list of rules for how they&nbsp;
should interact with British royalty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:10.480
Among them, they were forbidden from turning&nbsp;
their backs on the prince or princess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.200
and could not speak unless spoken to.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.560
Unfortunately, shortly after the movie began, Fox&nbsp;
realized he had to go to the bathroom and found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:24.720
himself in an uncomfortable position. He couldn't&nbsp;
walk away from Princess Diana without turning his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:29.680
back on her and he couldn't excuse himself&nbsp;
since he was instructed not to speak to her.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:35.200
"So the thing was, I sat in&nbsp;
total pain watching the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:40.000
with the Princess of Wales, and it was&nbsp;
great. It was, it was … She was smokin’ hot."

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:44.800
Before "Back to the Future" was&nbsp;
officially released on July 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:48.800
1985, a test audience got to&nbsp;
see the film when it first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:55.440
screened in May of 1985 at the Century Domes&nbsp;
Theater Complex in San Jose, California.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.320
Unlike a premiere, there were no signs or&nbsp;
hints suggesting what the movie would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.640
about. People did get excited when the&nbsp;
opening credits revealed the film was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:10.480
created by Steven Spielberg and Robert Zemeckis,&nbsp;
but many still had no idea what to expect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:17.040
Bob Gale recalled years later; when Doc Brown&nbsp;
sent his dog Einstein forward in time one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:21.360
minute causing him to vanish, some audience&nbsp;
members were distressed because they thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:26.080
the dog had been killed. Of course, these&nbsp;
viewers didn't realize the film was a comedy.

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:31.920
Today, when people think of "Back to the&nbsp;
Future," they think of the entire trilogy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.520
To a contemporary audience, it&nbsp;
appears that Zemeckis and Gale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.840
always planned the story to be an&nbsp;
epic three-part tale. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:44.640
the infamous words, "To Be Continued..."&nbsp;
appeared at the end of the first movie, right?

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:51.120
Except that, for audiences who saw the film in&nbsp;
1985, they didn't. The original flick did indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.240
end with Doc Brown taking Marty and his&nbsp;
girlfriend Jennifer Parker to the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:01.680
but neither Zemeckis nor Gale envisioned the&nbsp;
movie as anything more than a standalone. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:06.960
when the film became a huge hit, the studio put&nbsp;
pressure on the filmmakers to continue the story.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:11.520
As a result, when "Back to the Future"&nbsp;
was released on home video in 1986,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:17.360
MCA/Universal added the words, "To Be&nbsp;
Continued..." at the end. This helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:22.880
build anticipation among fans as well as new&nbsp;
viewers who saw the movie for the first time on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:29.840
VHS tape — which was helpful, since "Back to the&nbsp;
Future Part II" wouldn't hit theaters until 1989.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:34.320
In 2002, the "To Be Continued..."&nbsp;
ending was removed again when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.680
"Back to the Future" trilogy&nbsp;
was released as a DVD box set.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:43.520
"Back to the Future" might be considered&nbsp;
a sweet comedy by many fans, but it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:49.200
have some uncomfortable moments — particularly&nbsp;
the scenes between Marty McFly and his teenage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:54.000
mother Lorraine. After Marty is hit by the&nbsp;
car that was supposed to strike his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.560
Lorraine falls for him, unaware&nbsp;
that Marty is actually her own son.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:03.280
It was this subplot that caused the movie&nbsp;
to be turned down by Disney when Zemeckis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:08.000
and Gale were looking for a studio to&nbsp;
produce the film. Gale would later recall:

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.720
"We went in to meet with&nbsp;
an executive, and he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:15.680
'Are you guys nuts? Are you insane? We can't&nbsp;
make a movie like this. You've got the kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:21.040
and the mother in his car! It's incest —&nbsp;
this is Disney. It's too dirty for us!'"

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:26.800
Weirdly, the "incest" plot proved popular&nbsp;
with at least one audience member.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:32.640
In the documentary "Back in Time," Adam F.&nbsp;
Goldberg, creator of "The Goldbergs" TV show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:37.280
recalled that his mother came back from&nbsp;
the movie theater in 1985 and told him,

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:40.480
"I just saw this amazing movie&nbsp;
called Back to the Future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:44.000
It's about a boy who goes back&nbsp;
and falls in love with his mom."

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:49.040
Her review immediately turned off&nbsp;
the younger Goldberg for obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:54.000
reasons — although he later came around&nbsp;
and became a fan ... of the film, that is!

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:59.520
At its core, "Back to the Future" is a&nbsp;
movie about keeping families together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:04.480
This motivated a young Dan Harmon, co-creator&nbsp;
of the "Back to the Future"-influenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:08.880
adult animated series "Rick and Morty,"&nbsp;
to make his parents go see the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:14.080
because they were separated at the time. In&nbsp;
the documentary "Back in Time," Harmon said,

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:18.320
"Subconsciously, it was like, maybe&nbsp;
my parents will get back together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.000
if they watch this. They did get back together!"
 &nbsp;

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:28.640
As Huey Lewis famously sings on the "Back to the&nbsp;
Future" soundtrack, that's the power of love.

